(a) Any pawnbroker operating a business in the unincorporated areas of the County shall keep a book in which shall be legibly written, in ink or type, in the English language, at the time of each loan, purchase, sale, or receipt of personal property:
(1) The name of the person conducting the transaction;
(2) The number of the receipt or pawn ticket;
(3) If the payment is made by check, the number of the check issued for the purchase price or loan;
(4) The maturity date of the transaction;
(5) The signature of the customer;
(6) An accurate account and description of the goods, articles or things pawned, purchased or received;
(7) The amount of monies loaned or advanced thereon or paid therefor;
(8) The dates, and the times, including both days and hours of pawning, purchasing or receiving such goods, articles or things; and
(9) The names, age, residences and descriptions of the general appearance of the persons pawning, selling or delivering such goods, articles or things, and the distinctive number from the customer's driver's license or similar identification card.
No entry made in such book shall be erased, obliterated or defaced. The book required herein shall be kept and used for the sole and limited purpose of complying with this section.
(b) Every pawnbroker shall have a correct copy of all entries made in the book required by the preceding subsection during the immediately preceding 7-day period available for inspection at the place of business by the Sheriff's Department at 1:00 p.m. on Thursday of each week.
(c) The book required by subsection (a) of this section, as well as every article or thing pawned, pledged, sold or deposited, shall be open to inspection by the Sheriff's Department during normal business hours when the pawn shop is open to the public.
(d) No pawnbroker shall take in pawn, pledge or as security, or purchase, any article from any person under 18 years of age.
(e) If any person is found to be the owner of stolen property which has been pawned, pledged or sold to a pawnbroker, such property shall be returned by the pawnbroker to the owner thereof without the payment of the amount advanced by the pawnbroker or any costs or charges of any kind which the pawnbroker may have placed upon the same. Stolen property shall be surrendered to any Law Enforcement Officer only upon presentation of a police incident report which contains an accurate description of the stolen property sufficient to identify the property which has allegedly been stolen.
(Code 2005, § 38-5; Ord. of 4-12-1988, §§ 1—6)
